{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He became count at a young age after his mother Hodierna returned to Jerusalem and his father Raymond II was murdered by Assassins.\n\n*Reign* Raymond III was a skilled military leader and politician. He was involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a regent for King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. Raymond III was a member of the Haute Cour of Jerusalem the high court of the kingdom and played a significant role in the decision making process. He was also a leader of the opposition to the influence of the Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem and the Knights Templar.\n\nRaymond III was married to Eschiva of Bures the widow of Walter of Saint Omer Prince of Galilee. The marriage was childless and after his death the County of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV a minor who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. *Succession Crisis* The succession crisis that followed Raymond III's death was a significant event in the history of the County of Tripoli. The county was eventually taken over by Bohemond IV a younger son of Bohemond III who was also the count of Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was a leader of the faction that opposed the policy of Guy of Lusignan the king of Jerusalem and was involved in the negotiations with Saladin the Ayyubid sultan. The battle was a disaster for the Crusaders and resulted in the capture of King Guy and the loss of Jerusalem. Raymond III died soon after the battle and was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV. He was remembered a